          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "To determine if a three-character category can be used as a standalone code in the ICD-10 coding system, you can refer to the following guidelines:\n\n1. **Refer to the ICD-10 Guidelines**: The ICD-10 system has specific rules regarding which codes can stand alone. Categories that do not have further subclassifications and are fully specified can often be used alone.\n\n2. **Check for Fourth and Fifth Characters**: Many three-character categories require additional characters (fourth or fifth) to convey more detail about the condition. If a three-character code has specified additional characters in the coding guidelines, it cannot stand alone.\n\n3. **Consult the Tabular List**: The ICD-10 Tabular List organizes codes and will indicate if a three-character category can be used alone or requires additional characters for specificity. This list provides detailed information about the usage of each code.\n\n4. **Review Coding Clinics and Updates**: The American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) and similar organizations often publish updates and guidelines that can provide insight into standalone code usage.\n\nBy checking these resources, you can determine whether a three-character category can be used as a standalone code.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
